I think it is theoretically possible to estimate a random site effect in this case, since in each dataset there were a handful of sites that were sampled during two different periods. However I agree with Marc that there simply isn't enough data available to get good estimates of a random site effect - you would need more sites and more periods per site. Hence the various non-convergence errors.

